      "tryIt": "Vary the trade arrival rate mu from 10 to 100 shares/sec and plot the resulting change in expected fill time across queue depth.",
      "check": {
        "question": "In the Cont-Stoikov order book model, why does the queue depletion rate increase as the number of shares resting ahead increases?",
        "options": [
          "Because each resting order has an independent cancellation hazard rate; a larger queue has a higher aggregate cancellation intensity (theta * q_ahead).",
          "Because the exchange matching engine speeds up its clock frequency when order books are deep.",
          "Because the SEC mandates higher execution priority for retail orders in deep queues.",
          "Because market makers double their trade sizes when queues exceed 5,000 shares."
        ],
        "answer": 0,
        "why": "Every resting share has an independent probability of cancelling per unit time; hence, total cancellation rate equals theta * q_ahead, meaning larger queues experience higher aggregate cancellation volume."
      }
